        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
This experiment examined the effect of long-term exposure to microgravity (µG) and the effects of Extra Vehicular Activity (EVA) on pulmonary function.  A longitudinal study was performed of four crews of the International Space Station (ISS), measuring aspects of pulmonary function that may be affected by long-term exposure to µG per se, and by exposure to noxious gases, or particulate matter present in the atmosphere of the ISS.  The investigators proposed to evaluate the effect of EVA on the lung by studying those crewmembers who perform EVAs before and after single and repeated EVAs.  Crewmembers who did not perform EVAs served as a flying control group for this aspect of the study.  Because EVA poses a significant risk of decompression sickness including bubble events within the pulmonary circulation, non-invasive tests of pulmonary function that are altered by changes in the pulmonary vasculature presented an ideal way to follow a subject over the course of multiple EVAs.
<P>
To test the hypotheses researchers used the following: the standard respiratory function measurement of forced spirometry, the measurement of intra-breath respiratory exchange ratio (intrabreath R), a hyperventilation-breathhold measurement of the distribution of pulmonary perfusion, slow spirometry for lung volume subdivision, and the measurement of maximum inspiratory and expiratory pressures to test the hypotheses.
<P> 
APPROACH:<BR>
Each in-flight test session consisted of five non-invasive tests of lung function: (1) intra-breath respiratory exchange ratio, (2) distribution of pulmonary perfusion, (3) maximum inspiratory and expiratory pressures, (4) slow spirometry, and (5) maximum expiratory flow-volume.  Each of these tests involved breathing only cabin air.
<P> 
On the day of testing, the experiment system was set up and calibrated.  After the initial calibration, each subject performed a standard sequence of breathing tests.  After all subjects completed testing, a final calibration was performed and the data archived.  The system was then deconfigured and stowed.
<P> 
The first in-flight data collection was scheduled to occur two weeks into the mission, providing a data point comparable to the data already collected previously during several Spacelab flights.  Thereafter, data collection sessions occurred at approximately monthly intervals for the duration of each flight.  EVA-related in-flight data collection was performed as an experiment of opportunity.  All subjects performed a test session within one week prior to the EVA.  Following an EVA, the same session was repeated by those crewmembers who performed EVA, preferably on the same day that EVA was completed or, if necessary because of crew scheduling, on the day immediately following.  An abbreviated sequence (two breathing tests) could be implemented if time was limited on the day of EVA.  For subjects performing repeat EVAs (i.e., EVAs on successive days, or close to successive days), the abbreviated sequence was performed following every EVA in order to document the effect of repeated decompressions on the lung in µG.
<P> 
Testing was also conducted four times preflight and four times postflight.  Each of these sessions included the in-flight protocol and additional tests (diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide, single and multiple breath nitrogen washout, and cardiac output), measurements not possible in flight due to hardware limitations.  Hemoglobin level was also required once preflight and four times postflight.
<P> 
RESULTS:<BR>
Excellent quality data were collected from four Expeditions, comprising eight crewmembers who performed EVAs.  The investigators reported no significant changes in pulmonary gas exchange one day after an EVA was performed.  A small increase in metabolic rates in the astronauts was observed on the day following the EVA.  The investigators reported that testing was not ideal because acute effects resulting from EVAs could not be measured immediately after performing the spacewalk due to EVA protocols and logistics, but remain confident in their primary conclusion.  The small effect observed on the day following an EVA suggested that current protocols prevent the decompression stress associated with EVA from causing any major lasting disruption to gas exchange in the lung.  The results clearly showed that vital capacity in prolonged periods of µG is essentially unchanged compared to that measured standing and that respiratory muscle strength is maintained throughout approximately six months in µG.<end>
